---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudySection from "../../components/CaseStudySection.astro";

const meta = [
	{ label: "Timeline", value: "Oct 2025 - Jun 2026" },
	{ label: "Role", value: "Product Designer" },
	{ label: "Tools", value: "Figma, Magic Patterns, Figma Make" },
	{ label: "Deliverables", value: "UX research, Storefront Landing page" },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="Viable Community"
	heroSrc="/images/work/viable-community/hero.png"
	heroAlt="Viable Marketplace storefront homepage"
	meta={meta}
>
	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Outcome" heading="From no brand to a trust-first product card">
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-4 sm:flex-row">
			<img
				src="/images/work/viable-community/outcome-1.png"
				alt="Viable Marketplace storefront with the redesigned product cards"
				class="aspect-[630/479] w-full rounded-[4px] object-cover sm:w-[630px]"
			/>
			<img
				src="/images/work/viable-community/outcome-2.png"
				alt="Handmade soap product card showing ratings, sustainability score, and verified badge"
				class="aspect-[323/479] w-full rounded-[4px] object-contain sm:w-[323px]"
			/>
		</div>
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-xs text-[#1a1a1a]">The final changes to the marketplace.</p>
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			Research on 11 competitors and a validated persona became Viable's positioning statement,
			the thing every downstream design decision got tested against. The product card redesign
			that came out of it closed the trust gap directly: vague "eco-friendly" text claims became
			scannable, glanceable proof sized to work in under a second of shopper attention.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Problem" heading="Building brand identity from scratch">
		<img
			src="/images/work/viable-community/problem.png"
			alt="Research board covering competitor analysis and market positioning"
			class="aspect-[907/479] w-full rounded-[4px] object-cover"
		/>
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">No visual identity, no brand system.</strong> Viable Community had
				a rough idea of who they were selling to, but nothing concrete to design from.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">No audience data to anchor decisions.</strong> The lack of a demographic
				made it hard to know how to present their products.
			</p>
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="My Role" heading="Grounding the work in the market">
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			I conducted research on 4 competitors and was in charge of persona construction in Phase 1,
			I researched across four criteria: market and audience, sustainability and impact, business
			and trust model, and product and market experience, including Ecomondo, Wallapop, and
			Ethical Superstore. Then carried that foundation into Phase 2, translating findings into the
			storefront landing page and product cards and defending those decisions in stakeholder
			reviews.
		</p>
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-4 sm:flex-row">
			<img
				src="/images/work/viable-community/my-role-1.png"
				alt="Viable Marketplace storefront homepage"
				class="aspect-[630/479] w-full rounded-[4px] object-contain sm:w-[630px]"
			/>
			<img
				src="/images/work/viable-community/my-role-2.png"
				alt="Handmade soap product card showing ratings, sustainability score, and verified badge"
				class="aspect-[323/479] w-full rounded-[4px] object-contain sm:w-[323px]"
			/>
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Product Decisions" heading="Phase 1: Research & Persona Construction">
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			I conducted research on competitors and was in charge of persona construction in phase 1.
			Then carried that foundation into phase 2, translating findings into the storefront landing
			page and product cards, and defending those decisions in stakeholder reviews.
		</p>
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
			<img
				src="/images/work/viable-community/phase1-research.png"
				alt="Research criteria used to evaluate competitors"
				class="aspect-[960/540] w-full rounded-[4px] object-cover"
			/>
			<img
				src="/images/work/viable-community/phase1-competitors.png"
				alt="Competitive analysis comparing Ecomondo, Wallapop, and Ethical Superstore"
				class="aspect-[960/567] w-full rounded-[4px] object-cover"
			/>
		</div>
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			I researched across four criteria: market and audience, sustainability and impact, business
			and trust model, product and market experience, against eleven competitors including
			Ecomondo, Wallapop, and Ethical Superstore.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Product Decisions" heading="A positioning and a behavior to design around">
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				A positioning to build the brand voice around. Verified, affordable, community owned,
				trust first. Every visual and content decision in the redesign had to reinforce that
				Viable wasn't just another ethical marketplace, but one people could actually trust and
				afford.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				A behavior to build the interface around. People were scanning and comparing, not
				reading. That meant the storefront needed to work at a glance, not reward slow reading.
			</p>
		</div>
		<img
			src="/images/work/viable-community/positioning-statement.png"
			alt="Product positioning statement summary"
			class="aspect-[960/540] w-full rounded-[4px] object-cover"
		/>
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			<strong class="font-bold">A real market gap.</strong> Sustainable platforms were fragmented by
			category, sustainability itself was priced as a premium, impact claims were hard to trust
			because of greenwashing, and no platform gave its community real ownership or a voice.
		</p>
		<img
			src="/images/work/viable-community/market-gap-diagram.png"
			alt="Diagram illustrating the market gap in sustainable marketplaces"
			class="aspect-[960/540] w-full rounded-[4px] object-contain"
		/>
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">A positioning to build the brand voice around.</strong> Verified, affordable,
				community-owned, trust-first. Every visual and content decision in the redesign had to
				reinforce that Viable wasn't just another ethical marketplace, but one people could
				actually trust and afford.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">A behavior to build the interface around.</strong> People were scanning
				and comparing, not reading. That meant the storefront needed to work at a glance, not reward
				slow reading.
			</p>
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Phase 2 solution" heading="Card shape">
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			The research pointed to a trust problem: vague "eco-friendly" claims with no evidence, read
			by shoppers who scanned instead of reading.
		</p>
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-4 sm:flex-row">
			<div class="flex flex-1 flex-col items-center gap-2">
				<p class="font-space-grotesk text-2xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a]">Before</p>
				<img
					src="/images/work/viable-community/card-before.png"
					alt="Original product card design with description, certifications, and seller bio"
					class="aspect-[514/479] w-full rounded-[4px] object-contain"
				/>
			</div>
			<div class="flex flex-1 flex-col items-center gap-2">
				<p class="font-space-grotesk text-2xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a]">After</p>
				<img
					src="/images/work/viable-community/card-after.png"
					alt="Redesigned product card with a star rating and sustainability score"
					class="aspect-[419/479] w-full rounded-[4px] object-contain"
				/>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">Tried:</strong> a card with a description, certifications, and a seller
				bio for more information to prove trust.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">Result:</strong> it read as more credible in isolation, but fell apart
				in context. Next to nine other cards in a scroll, the extra text slowed the scan down.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">Fix:</strong> cut to two signals readable in under a second and comparable
				at card size, a star rating and a leaf based sustainability score.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">Kept:</strong> seller attribution, but smaller, for provenance without
				competing for attention.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">Moved:</strong> everything else to the product detail page, where someone
				who's already stopped scanning is willing to read. The card ended up deliberately smaller
				than a "complete" listing, built for the fifteenth item in a scroll, not the first.
			</p>
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Product Decisions" heading="Smaller fixes that added up">
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			The research pointed to a trust problem: vague "eco-friendly" claims with no evidence, read
			by shoppers who scanned instead of reading.
		</p>
		<div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-2">
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-2xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a]">Before</p>
			<img
				src="/images/work/viable-community/webfixes-before.png"
				alt="Original storefront header with dual CTAs, no logo, and grey category pills"
				class="aspect-[961/585] w-full rounded-[4px] object-contain"
			/>
		</div>
		<div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-2">
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-2xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a]">After</p>
			<img
				src="/images/work/viable-community/webfixes-after.png"
				alt="Redesigned storefront header with a logo, single primary CTA, and free shipping utility bar"
				class="aspect-[961/535] w-full rounded-[4px] object-cover"
			/>
		</div>
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				An audit of the existing storefront turned up smaller issues working against the trust
				and glanceability goals, the things a shopper registers before they ever reach a product
				card:
			</p>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6">
				<li class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
					<strong class="font-bold">Dual primary CTAs.</strong> "Sell an item" and "Log in / Sign in"
					competed for attention with no hierarchy, so I demoted "Sell an item" to a secondary outline
					style.
				</li>
				<li class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
					<strong class="font-bold">No logo.</strong> A header that already took up a disproportionate
					share of the screen had no brand mark, so I added one.
				</li>
				<li class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
					<strong class="font-bold">No category pills.</strong> Every category sat in its own grey pill,
					which read as clutter rather than navigation, so I flattened it into a simpler tab pattern.
				</li>
				<li class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
					<strong class="font-bold">No incentive signal.</strong> Nothing up top gave shoppers a reason
					to trust the price, so I added a utility bar surfacing the free shipping threshold.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Outcome" heading="What I learned">
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				I contributed the card redesign and the Phase 1 research behind it, then stepped back
				from most of Phase 2 when the unpaid workload outpaced my bandwidth. I don't have full
				visibility into the site's final production status beyond my own contributions, but the
				work didn't need me in the room to stay correct. That's the real test of research: not
				whether it holds up in the room where you defend it, but whether it still holds up in the
				room you're not in.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				The lesson for next time is about scope, not craft. Push for a defined handoff before
				bandwidth runs out, not after.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="aspect-[960/479] w-full rounded-[4px] bg-[#4a4a4a]"></div>
	</CaseStudySection>
</CaseStudyLayout>
